### Part (a): Checking if a string is in \( L \)

The language \( L \) consists of strings that are concatenations of a word with itself, i.e., \( ww \). To check if a string \( x \) is in \( L \), we need to determine if it can be split into two equal halves.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int is_in_L(const char *x) {
    size_t len = strlen(x);
    if (len % 2 != 0) return 0; // Length must be even

    size_t half_len = len / 2;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < half_len; i++) {
        if (x[i] != x[i + half_len]) {
            return 0; // Halves are not equal
        }
    }
    return 1; // String is in L
}
```

### Part (b): Checking if a string is in \( L^* \)

The language \( L^* \) consists of the empty string and all strings formed by concatenating zero or more strings from \( L \). To check if a string \( y \) is in \( L^* \), we need to see if it can be segmented into substrings, each of which is in \( L \).

We can use a greedy approach to minimize the number of calls to `is_in_L`. We will try to find the longest possible prefix of \( y \) that is in \( L \), remove it, and repeat the process until \( y \) is empty.

```c
int is_in_L_star(const char *y) {
    size_t len = strlen(y);
    size_t pos = 0;

    while (pos < len) {
        size_t max_half_len = (len - pos) / 2;
        if (max_half_len == 0) return 0; // No more possible splits

        // Try all possible splits
        for (size_t half_len = max_half_len; half_len > 0; half_len--) {
