To ensure that Hibernate uses the exact table names as specified in the `@Table` annotation (including case sensitivity), you need to configure the **physical naming strategy** in your Hibernate setup. By default, Hibernate may transform names to lowercase or uppercase depending on the database and configuration.

Here’s how you can achieve this:

### 1. **Use `PhysicalNamingStrategyStandardImpl` with `ImplicitNamingStrategyLegacyComponentPathImpl`**

This combination ensures that Hibernate uses the exact names as specified in your annotations without altering the case.

```java
import org.hibernate.boot.model.naming.CamelCaseToUnderscoresNamingStrategy;
import org.hibernate.boot.model.naming.Identifier;
import org.hibernate.boot.model.naming.PhysicalNamingStrategyStandardImpl;
import org.hibernate.engine.jdbc.env.spi.JdbcEnvironment;
import org.springframework.boot.orm.jpa.hibernate.SpringPhysicalNamingStrategy;

public class CustomPhysicalNamingStrategy extends PhysicalNamingStrategyStandardImpl {

    @Override
    public Identifier toPhysicalCatalogName(Identifier name, JdbcEnvironment context) {
        return toPhysicalName(name, context);
    }

    @Override
    public Identifier toPhysicalSchemaName(Identifier name, JdbcEnvironment context) {
        return toPhysicalName(name, context);
    }

    @Override
    public Identifier toPhysicalTableName(Identifier name, JdbcEnvironment context) {
        return toPhysicalName(name, context);
    }

    @Override
    public Identifier toPhysicalSequenceName(Identifier name, JdbcEnvironment context) {
        return toPhysicalName(name, context);
    }

    @Override
    public Identifier toPhysicalColumnName(Identifier name, JdbcEnvironment context) {
        return toPhysicalName(name, context);
    }

    private Identifier toPhysicalName(Identifier name, JdbcEnvironment context) {
        if (name == null) {
            return null;
        }
        return new Identifier(name.getText(), name.isQuoted());
    }
}
```

**Spring Boot Configuration:**

Add the following to your `application.properties` or `application.yml`:

**Properties:**
```properties
spring.jpa.hibernate.naming.physical-strategy=com.example.CustomPhysicalNamingStrategy
spring.jpa.hibernate.naming.implicit-strategy=org.hibernate.boot.model.naming.ImplicitNamingStrategyLegacyComponentPathImpl
```

**YAML:**
```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      naming:
        physical-strategy: com.example.CustomPhysicalNamingStrategy
        implicit-strategy: org.hibernate.boot.model.naming.ImplicitNamingStrategyLegacyComponentPathImpl
```

### 2. **Use `SpringPhysicalNamingStrategy` (Spring Boot Specific)**

Spring Boot provides a `SpringPhysicalNamingStrategy` that respects the `@Table` annotation's name as-is.

**Properties:**
