To persist an `Employee` object to an H2 database using Hibernate, you need to follow these steps:

1. **Add Dependencies**: Include Hibernate and H2 dependencies in your `pom.xml` if you're using Maven.
2. **Create the Entity Class**: Define the `Employee` class as a Hibernate entity.
3. **Configure Hibernate**: Set up the `hibernate.cfg.xml` file or use Java-based configuration.
4. **Persist the Object**: Write code to save the `Employee` object to the database.

Below is a complete example:

### 1. `pom.xml` Dependencies

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- Hibernate Core -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.hibernate</groupId>
        <artifactId>hibernate-core</artifactId>
        <version>5.6.10.Final</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- H2 Database -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
        <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
        <version>2.1.210</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

### 2. `Employee.java` Entity Class

```java
import javax.persistence.Entity;
import javax.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import javax.persistence.GenerationType;
import javax.persistence.Id;

@Entity
public class Employee {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String firstName;
    private String lastName;
    private String email;

    // Constructors, Getters, and Setters

    public Employee() {
    }

    public Employee(String firstName, String lastName, String email) {
        this.firstName = firstName;
        this.lastName = lastName;
        this.email = email;
    }

    // Getters and Setters

    public Long getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public void setId(Long id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getFirstName() {
        return firstName;
    }

    public void setFirstName(String firstName) {
        this.firstName = firstName;
    }

    public String getLastName() {
        return lastName;
    }

    public void setLastName(String lastName) {
        this.lastName = lastName;
    }

    public String getEmail() {
        return email;
    }

    public void setEmail(String email) {
        this.email = email;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Employee{" +
                "id=" + id +
                ", firstName='" + firstName + '\'' +
                ", lastName='" + lastName + '\'' +
                ", email='" + email + '\'' +
                '}';
    }
}
```

### 3. `hibernate.cfg.xml` Configuration

Place this file in the `src/main/resources` directory.

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE hibernate-configuration PUBLIC
        "-//Hibernate/Hibernate Configuration DTD 3.0//EN"
        "http://www.hibernate.org/dtd/hibernate-configuration-3.0.dtd">

<hibernate-configuration>
    <session-factory>
        <!-- Database connection settings -->
        <property name="connection.driver_class">org.h2.Driver</property>
        <property name="connection.url">jdbc:h2:mem:testdb</property>
        <property name="connection.username">sa</property>
        <property name="connection.password"></property>

        <!-- SQL dialect -->
        <property name="dialect">org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect</property>

        <!-- Enable Hibernate's automatic session context management -->
        <property name="current_session_context_class">thread</property>

        <!-- Drop and re-create the database schema on startup -->
        <property name="hbm2ddl.auto">create-drop</property>

        <!-- Enable SQL logging -->
        <property name="show_sql">true</property>

        <!-- Mapped classes -->
        <mapping class="com.example.Employee"/>
    </session-factory>
</hibernate-configuration>
```

### 4. Persisting the Employee Object

```java
import org.hibernate.Session;
import org.hibernate.SessionFactory;
import org.hibernate.cfg.Configuration;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a SessionFactory from hibernate.cfg.xml
        SessionFactory sessionFactory = new Configuration()
                .configure("hibernate.cfg.xml")
                .addAnnotatedClass(Employee.class)
                .buildSessionFactory();

        // Create a Session
        Session session = sessionFactory.getCurrentSession();

        try {
            // Begin a transaction
            session.beginTransaction();

            // Create an Employee object
            Employee employee = new Employee("John", "Doe", "john.doe@example.com");

            // Persist the Employee object
            session.save(employee);

            // Commit the transaction
            session.getTransaction().commit();

            System.out.println("Employee saved successfully: " + employee);

        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Roll back the transaction in case of an error
            if (session.getTransaction() != null) {
                session.getTransaction().rollback();
            }
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            // Close the SessionFactory
            sessionFactory.close();
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:

- **Hibernate Configuration**: The `hibernate.cfg.xml` file configures the database connection, dialect, and other Hibernate properties.
- **Entity Class**: The `Employee` class is annotated with `@Entity` to mark it as a Hibernate entity. The `@Id` annotation marks the primary key.
- **Persisting Object**: The `Main` class demonstrates how to create a `SessionFactory`, start a session, begin a transaction, save an `Employee` object, and commit the transaction.

### Running the Code:
When you run the `Main` class, Hibernate will create the necessary tables in the H2 in-memory database and persist the `Employee` object. The SQL statements executed by Hibernate will be logged to the console due to the `show_sql` property.

This example uses an in-memory H2 database, so the data will be lost when the application stops. For a persistent database, you can change the `connection.url` property to point to a file-based H2 database or any other database supported by Hibernate.

